graph cycle detector
==================

Write a function:

    def graph_cycle_detector(graph: dict[int, list[int]]) -> bool

The graph is a DIRECTED adjacency map: each key is a node and its value is
the list of nodes it points to. Return True if the graph contains a cycle,
False otherwise. A node that appears only as a neighbor may be missing
from the keys - treat it as having no outgoing edges. The graph may be
disconnected; an empty graph has no cycle.

Examples:
    graph_cycle_detector({0: [1], 1: [2], 2: [0]}) -> True
    graph_cycle_detector({0: [1, 2], 1: [2], 2: []}) -> False
    graph_cycle_detector({0: [0]}) -> True
    graph_cycle_detector({0: [1], 1: [], 2: [3], 3: [2]}) -> True
    graph_cycle_detector({0: [1, 2], 1: [3], 2: [3], 3: []}) -> False
    graph_cycle_detector({}) -> False
